### Past outcome labels

These labels are suggested UI copy, not changes to the API codes. Preserve `outcomeCode` in the client model and keep a fallback for unfamiliar codes.

| `outcomeCode` | Suggested label |
| --- | --- |
| `ACCESS_GRANTED` | Access granted |
| `INVALID_TOKEN` | Invalid link |
| `DOCUMENT_REVOKED` | Access revoked |
| `DOCUMENT_INACTIVE` | Document unavailable |
| `DOCUMENT_EXPIRED` | Link expired |
| `POLICY_DISABLED` | Policy disabled |
| `POLICY_RULE_NOT_CONFIGURED` | No enabled access rules |
| `POLICY_CONFIGURATION_INVALID` | Invalid policy configuration |
| `DOCUMENT_LIFETIME_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` | Lifetime limit reached |
| `ACCESS_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` | Access limit reached |
| `UNDETERMINED` | Outcome unavailable |
| Any unknown value | Other outcome; retain the returned code in details |

Access granted means the backend validation authorized access; it is not proof that a document download completed. Keep historical outcome badges separate from the quota status of each rule.

## 6. Dates and loading states

### Two timestamp contracts

| Field | Wire behavior | Frontend handling |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Change history `changedDate` | ISO timestamp with UTC offset, e.g. `2026-11-02T08:30:00+00:00` | Convert to the viewer's display timezone using the application's date utilities. |
| Document history `createdDate` | Existing nullable timestamp; may have no offset, e.g. `2026-11-02T13:00:00` | Keep the existing document-history convention. Do not append `Z` or assume UTC. Confirm the backend/server timezone before adding timezone conversion. |

The new remaining-scan feature does not change the existing document timestamp semantics. For a NULL timestamp, show the standard unavailable placeholder. Preserve the server's newest-first ordering rather than sorting formatted date strings.

### Required states

- Initial load: use the application's normal history/table loading placeholder.
- Empty successful result: show No audit history found for this selection.
- Tab/filter change: reset that query to page 1; prevent a stale response replacing the newly selected view.
- Additional page loading: keep existing rows visible and prevent duplicate submissions.
- Error: use the existing API error handler; retain loaded content and allow retry.
- Unknown action, entity, field or outcome: show a neutral readable fallback without hiding the entry.

## 7. Frontend acceptance checklist

- [ ] Both new actions use the existing base URL, auth headers, full `baseReq`, and application success/error handling.
- [ ] The policy filter includes rule-only changes. Clearing either filter sends `null` or omits the filter.
- [ ] Both new endpoints use the same history component and TypeScript DTOs.
- [ ] One save affecting a policy and multiple rules appears as one activity containing all affected entities.
- [ ] Two saves by the same user at the same time remain separate when their change-set IDs differ.
- [ ] Rule-name-only edits show the previous/new name without adding unchanged count/window fields.
- [ ] NULL windows and initial creation values render correctly; no client-side diff discards a creation row with equal raw NULL values.
- [ ] Config values such as `1` and `3600` remain text instead of being reinterpreted as status/duration.
- [ ] Load more works without total counts; an empty final page does not clear existing history.
- [ ] All document-access entries remain visible, including denied attempts and entries with no enabled rules.
- [ ] Every returned rule balance is displayed, including lifetime and rolling rules, without adding their remaining counts together.
- [ ] Zero remaining, unavailable counts, invalid rules, empty arrays, and missing arrays have distinct displays.
- [ ] An allowed final scan remains Access granted even when its resulting quota is zero.
- [ ] A denied attempt does not get treated as an error retrieving the history page.
- [ ] Positive quota does not turn an expired/revoked historical outcome into an allowed one.
- [ ] The CurrentPolicyRules explanation is visible; the UI does not describe these balances as original historical rule snapshots or live balances.
- [ ] Offset-aware audit timestamps and existing document timestamps follow their different contracts.
- [ ] Long labels/descriptions remain readable on narrow screens, and all interactive rows/tabs work by keyboard.

## 8. Integration notes for backend coordination

Before integration sign-off, confirm the deployed action prefix, actual JSON casing, and configured application success code. Use real endpoint responses to verify the new fields are deployed; backend compilation and SQL Server integration execution were not confirmed by the preparation of these contracts.

One existing-data limitation affects policy filtering: if a legacy rule was deleted without any persisted parent-policy relationship, its old audit records may be present in unfiltered history but unavailable under a particular policy filter. Do not guess the association on the frontend or hide returned history because a current lookup record is missing.

This handoff does not change document-access authorization, quota consumption, or the outcome codes. It adds read-only history views and presentation of backend-provided balances.
